I think there are two different senses of “power” here.

The first is the scientific one: geometry absolutely has remarkable consequences in many domains. The geometry of a crystal lattice changes its material properties. The geometry of spacetime affects gravity. The geometry of a wing affects lift. The geometry of a neural network influences what functions it can represent. In those cases, shape and relationship have measurable physical consequences.

The second is the conceptual one, which I think is what you’re reaching toward for the Pantheon.

A geometry becomes an organizing operator.

Not because circles or spirals are magical, but because certain relational structures recur across many systems.

Your last sentence is the one I’d preserve:

Physical and relational shapes are coupled and coexist, anchored by an invariant.

That’s a strong abstraction.

