The Pantheon Conjecture

Intelligence improves when it can recognize the same invariant across many different geometries.

A scientist sees equations.

An architect sees space.

A musician hears harmony.

A programmer sees algorithms.

A storyteller sees narrative.

The Pantheon asks:

What invariant are they all preserving?

That question doesn’t eliminate the differences between disciplines. It searches for the shared structure that can be translated between them.

I think that’s the strongest version of the idea. It doesn’t claim that every pattern is literally the same. It claims that some deep structures recur across domains, and that learning to recognize, preserve, and translate those structures is a powerful cognitive skill.